.TH loadpng 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
loadpng \- draw a PNG on the screen, or turn one into a sprite
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loadpng
.I file
[\fIx\fR [\fIy\fR [\fItransparent\fR [\fIcutoff\fR]]]]
.br
.B loadpng \-s
.I file
[\fItransparent\fR [\fIcutoff\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B loadpng
decodes a PNG and draws it at
.IR "x y" ,
0 0 by default.
.PP
With
.BR \-s ,
it writes a SPRITE to standard output instead of drawing anything.  This
is how a BASIC program gets its sprites: decode once, redirect the
result to a file, and load that.  A sprite needs no display, so
.B \-s
works with the screen in any mode.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I transparent
The palette index to treat as transparent, 0 to 15.  When drawing,
.B \-1
means none, and is the default.
.IP
In the sprite form a
.I negative
value is MMBasic's sign trick: it substitutes that index for opaque
black and makes index 0 the transparent one.
.TP
.I cutoff
The alpha level, 1 to 254, at or below which a pixel counts as
transparent.  The default is 20 when drawing and 30 for a sprite,
matching what MMBasic uses for
.B LOAD PNG
and
.B SPRITE LOADPNG
respectively.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# loadpng logo.png 40 20
# loadpng logo.png 0 0 -1
# loadpng -s logo.png > logo.spr
.fi
.SH NOTES
The PNG decoder works in the PSRAM arena.  A machine without PSRAM
cannot run it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR loadjpg (1),
.BR loadimage (1),
.BR saveimage (1)
